WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.260
Voltage questions become much easier once the learner hears potential difference as a per-carrier story.

2
00:00:05.260 --> 00:00:10.520
A source gives each coulomb of charge a certain energy boost.

3
00:00:10.520 --> 00:00:15.780
That is what the voltage measures.

4
00:00:15.780 --> 00:00:21.040
It is not total energy, and it is not current.

5
00:00:21.040 --> 00:00:26.300
The most common electrical sentence to defeat here is the battery gives twelve amps when the label is actually twelve volts.

6
00:00:26.300 --> 00:00:31.560
The lesson becomes far more stable once voltage is read as joules per coulomb from the start.

7
00:00:31.560 --> 00:00:36.820
Keep this takeaway in view: Lesson 3 becomes safer once the learner can say joules per coulomb before touching the symbols.

8
00:00:36.820 --> 00:00:42.080
Voltage means energy per coulomb.

9
00:00:42.080 --> 00:00:47.340
A volt tells you how much energy each coulomb receives or transfers.

10
00:00:47.340 --> 00:00:52.600
Source role means boost per carrier.

11
00:00:52.600 --> 00:00:57.860
The source gives each moving carrier an energy lift around the loop.

12
00:00:57.860 --> 00:01:04.499
This diagram matters because it makes the per-carrier meaning visible.

13
00:01:04.499 --> 00:01:11.138
The source is shown as a lift station that boosts each carrier by a certain amount of energy.

14
00:01:11.138 --> 00:01:17.776
The important point is that the label belongs to each coulomb, not to the whole loop at once.

15
00:01:17.776 --> 00:01:24.415
That is why voltage is best read as energy per unit charge.

16
00:01:24.415 --> 00:01:31.054
Once the learner sees that, the route from V equals E over Q to E equals V Q becomes much easier to justify.

17
00:01:31.054 --> 00:01:37.692
Keep this takeaway in view: A strong voltage answer keeps the energy boost attached to each coulomb that passes the source.

18
00:01:37.692 --> 00:01:44.331
Carrier tokens: Each token stands for one moving unit of charge passing the source.

19
00:01:44.331 --> 00:01:50.970
Energy boost: The source label tells how much energy each coulomb gains.

20
00:01:50.970 --> 00:01:57.325
A ski-lift analogy helps because it separates the per-rider gain from the total number of riders.

21
00:01:57.325 --> 00:02:03.680
If every rider is lifted by the same height, the lift has delivered the same gain per rider.

22
00:02:03.680 --> 00:02:10.035
But the total energy involved can still change if more riders go through.

23
00:02:10.035 --> 00:02:16.390
Voltage works in the same way.

24
00:02:16.390 --> 00:02:22.745
It tells you the energy transfer per coulomb, while the total transferred energy depends on both the voltage and how much charge actually moves.

25
00:02:22.745 --> 00:02:29.100
Keep this takeaway in view: The analogy helps the learner defend why voltage is per charge rather than total energy or current.

26
00:02:29.100 --> 00:02:35.455
Per rider gain means voltage idea.

27
00:02:35.455 --> 00:02:41.810
The energy or height gain per rider matches joules per coulomb.

28
00:02:41.810 --> 00:02:48.165
Number of riders means charge amount.

29
00:02:48.165 --> 00:02:54.520
The total transferred energy depends on how many riders or coulombs pass through.

30
00:02:54.520 --> 00:03:00.112
If voltage is energy per unit charge, then V equals E over Q is the direct symbolic statement.

31
00:03:00.112 --> 00:03:05.705
Reversing the same idea gives E equals V Q when the total transferred energy is the unknown.

32
00:03:05.705 --> 00:03:11.297
The important habit is to say the meaning out loud while calculating.

33
00:03:11.297 --> 00:03:16.890
Nine volts means nine joules per coulomb.

34
00:03:16.890 --> 00:03:22.482
Twelve volts means twelve joules per coulomb.

35
00:03:22.482 --> 00:03:28.075
That spoken meaning prevents the learner from turning voltage into just another letter in an equation.

36
00:03:28.075 --> 00:03:33.668
Keep this takeaway in view: The formula scene works once the learner can explain what one volt means in ordinary language.

37
00:03:33.668 --> 00:03:39.260
Direction one: Known energy and charge give the potential difference.

38
00:03:39.260 --> 00:03:44.852
Direction two: Known voltage and charge give the total transferred energy.

39
00:03:44.852 --> 00:03:50.445
V = E / Q.

40
00:03:50.445 --> 00:03:56.038
E = V Q.

41
00:03:56.038 --> 00:04:01.630
Keep the phrase energy per charge alive while rearranging or substituting.

42
00:04:01.630 --> 00:04:07.766
A strong routine for this lesson has three steps.

43
00:04:07.766 --> 00:04:13.902
First identify the per-coulomb story and say the voltage in joules per coulomb.

44
00:04:13.902 --> 00:04:20.038
Second decide whether the question is asking for the per-charge value or the total transferred energy.

45
00:04:20.038 --> 00:04:26.174
Third divide or multiply accordingly using V equals E over Q or E equals V Q.

46
00:04:26.174 --> 00:04:32.310
This routine prevents voltage from collapsing into current language and gives the learner a very stable path through the worked example.

47
00:04:32.310 --> 00:04:38.446
Keep this takeaway in view: Per charge first, unknown second, operation third is the safest routine for Lesson 3.

48
00:04:38.446 --> 00:04:44.582
Step 1 means name the per-charge meaning.

49
00:04:44.582 --> 00:04:50.718
Translate volts into energy per coulomb before writing the relation.

50
00:04:50.718 --> 00:04:56.854
Step 2 means decide the unknown.

51
00:04:56.854 --> 00:05:02.990
Check whether the question wants voltage or total energy.

52
00:05:02.990 --> 00:05:07.835
A very common weak sentence says the battery gives twelve amps when the source is actually labeled twelve volts.

53
00:05:07.835 --> 00:05:12.679
This matters because volts and amps answer different questions.

54
00:05:12.679 --> 00:05:17.524
The voltage tells you the energy transfer per coulomb.

55
00:05:17.524 --> 00:05:22.368
The current tells you the rate at which charge flows.

56
00:05:22.368 --> 00:05:27.213
A source can keep the same voltage while different connected circuits produce different currents.

57
00:05:27.213 --> 00:05:32.057
That one comparison is enough to show why the labels must be kept apart.

58
00:05:32.057 --> 00:05:36.902
Keep this takeaway in view: Lesson 3 stays much safer once the learner refuses to use volts and amps interchangeably.

59
00:05:36.902 --> 00:05:41.746
Weak claim means 12 V becomes 12 A.

60
00:05:41.746 --> 00:05:46.591
This swaps the source's voltage label with the loop's current result.

61
00:05:46.591 --> 00:05:51.435
Correction means voltage is per charge.

62
00:05:51.435 --> 00:05:56.280
Keep volts on the energy-per-coulomb side of the story.

63
00:05:56.280 --> 00:06:00.846
Run four checks.

64
00:06:00.846 --> 00:06:05.413
Voltage means energy transferred per coulomb.

65
00:06:05.413 --> 00:06:09.979
One volt means one joule per coulomb.

66
00:06:09.979 --> 00:06:14.545
V equals E over Q gives the voltage when energy and charge are known, and E equals V Q gives the total transferred energy when voltage and charge are known.

67
00:06:14.545 --> 00:06:19.112
And voltage should never be described as current.

68
00:06:19.112 --> 00:06:23.678
Those four checks capture the lesson structure before the worked example begins.

69
00:06:23.678 --> 00:06:28.245
Keep this takeaway in view: The worked example is ready once the learner can explain why 9 V means 9 J for each coulomb.

70
00:06:28.245 --> 00:06:32.811
Meaning means energy per coulomb.

71
00:06:32.811 --> 00:06:37.377
This is the first sentence to say before using the formula.

72
00:06:37.377 --> 00:06:41.944
Voltage relation means V = E / Q.

73
00:06:41.944 --> 00:06:46.510
Use this when the per-charge quantity is unknown.

74
00:06:46.510 --> 00:06:53.609
Start by translating the given voltage into joules per coulomb or by recognizing that the given energy and charge will combine to make a joules-per-coulomb value.

75
00:06:53.609 --> 00:07:00.708
Then use V equals E over Q or E equals V Q depending on the unknown.

76
00:07:00.708 --> 00:07:07.807
Finish by restating the meaning in words: each coulomb gets a certain energy transfer.

77
00:07:07.807 --> 00:07:14.906
That is the cleanest route into the worked example and the best shield against mixing volts with amps.

78
00:07:14.906 --> 00:07:22.005
Keep this takeaway in view: Lesson 3 improves immediately once voltage is kept on the energy-per-charge side of the electrical story.

79
00:07:22.005 --> 00:07:29.104
Do not skip the unit meaning: Joules per coulomb should be heard before any substitution happens.

80
00:07:29.104 --> 00:07:36.203
Do not skip the final sentence: Explain what the result means for each coulomb or for the total transferred energy.

81
00:07:36.203 --> 00:07:43.302
read voltage as energy per coulomb.

82
00:07:43.302 --> 00:07:50.401
use V = E / Q or E = V Q depending on the unknown.

83
00:07:50.401 --> 00:07:57.500
The best voltage answers still sound physical after the arithmetic is finished.
